# Product review rules PC01–PC15

Use this maintained English guidance for sections 1–7 and 9 of the [frozen
Chinese product review export](PC规则.md). Its [provenance](PC规则.provenance.json)
identifies `产品SOP自检研究/产品SOP自检方案.md`, the source digest, and the selected
sections and citations. Use the frozen source for original quotations and
machine catalog bindings. Apply the [common contract](共同约定.md), current
applicability and version supplement (`.formsy-sop/skills/formsy-sop-workflow/references/sop-rules-addendum-20261016/applicability-and-version.md`), and effective
project decisions.

## 1. Product review scope

Check whether the SOP accurately records the user's problem, explains how the
user completes the task, and aligns features, interaction, data, and
acceptance. Identify product tradeoffs that need decisions. Technical review
checks implementation feasibility, modules, interfaces, data, and environment.
Connect the two reviews through requirement, feature, and acceptance IDs and
record their findings independently.

The historical Q checklist covers product concerns through Q07/Q08/Q09/Q13/Q16/Q21.
Use the following PC rules to expand applicability, satisfaction conditions,
evidence, findings, and recheck. The provenance retains the historical
checklist citation.

### 1.1 Conclusions and evidence

| Conclusion | Question | Basis and boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Product document review | Are the required objects complete, consistent, understandable, and ready for handoff? | Current document, rules, sources, and evidence for each level. Record actual product acceptance separately. |
| Technical document review | Are implementation design, handoff, and feasibility ready? | Independent technical rules and their required facts or trials. Use the technical result for technical readiness. |
| Product claim support | What supports claims about users, problems, tasks, or outcomes? | Original wording, applicable research or observation, with sample, environment, limits, and unverified assumptions. |
| Stage confirmation | Has the user accepted this version's goals, requirements, or plan? | Effective user decision for the corresponding stage and version. |
| Actual product acceptance | Does this build meet formal conditions in the target environment? | Actual operations, files, logs, screenshots, and assertions. Attribute design, prototype, and build checks separately. |

Use the user's explicit requirements as the design basis for their personal
tool. For a product serving external users, record the evidence level for
claims about need, efficiency, or willingness to pay. Establish commercial
claims with evidence appropriate to the claim.

### 1.2 Three review levels

| Level | Method | Supported conclusion | Scope limit |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| L1 structural check | Check fields, placeholders, IDs, references, covered objects, and versions with repeatable rules. | Results of the actual structural rules checked. | Evaluate need, usability, outcomes, and trials separately. |
| L2 product content review | Walk goals, tasks, pages, features, states, and acceptance against original requirements. Trace new behavior backward and verify relevant external facts. | Behavior consistency, gaps, conflicts, and design judgments within the evidence. | Obtain real evidence for user comprehension, scenario coverage, commercial claims, and approval. |
| L3 verification, research, or prototype evidence | Record actual interviews, observation, document walkthroughs, prototype tasks, or target-environment trials against a predefined question. | Results for the stated method, sample, and environment. | Limit results to exercised tasks, users, and platforms. |

Declare the required levels for every check. Use official sources for the facts
they state. Use actual user or prototype work when required and mark a planned
trial unexecuted. Attribute model-generated personas, simulated users, and
model review as proposals or review activity.

Use `satisfied`, `unsatisfied`, `unverified`, or `not_applicable` with rationale.
Record actual research and trial observations, results, and limits separately.
For application tests record passed, failed, unverified, or not applicable with
rationale. An applicable check is satisfied when all required levels have
supporting evidence.

## 2. Inputs and outputs

| Object | Minimum record |
| --- | --- |
| Product baseline | Project, document and rule versions, stage, user decisions, users, scope, platforms, original locators, and content identity. |
| Users and tasks | Known users and scenarios, tasks, current problems, intended outcomes, and classification as fact, suggestion, assumption, or unknown. |
| Product design | Scope and priority, journeys, applicable pages and entry points, feature cards, state rules, data, and permissions. |
| Acceptance design | Requirement and acceptance IDs, conditions, environment, data, operations, observable assertions, evidence plan, and platform applicability. |
| Check inventory | PC01–PC15, object set, applicability, required levels, satisfaction conditions, and evidence. |
| Research or prototype card | Question and hypothesis, method, sample or environment, authorization, preset judgment criteria, actual observations, results, and limitations. |
| Issue log | Issue, locator, impact, severity, blocker, owner, next action, recheck, and history. |
| Handoff | Product object to technical object, task, and acceptance mapping, conflicts, decisions, stage confirmation, and impact. |

Use the applicable feature and journey cards from the locale-matched Product
SOP template. Preserve existing IDs and formal wording. Use the technical
self-check method for mapping to technical objects. Product document review
can proceed before implementation where its required evidence is available.

## 3. Seven review steps

### 1. Pin versions, objects, and applicability

Read the product baseline, originals, decisions, platforms, and scope. Fix
the rule version and inventories for requirements, tasks, features, pages,
states, and acceptance. Review a draft with its draft status visible.

Use actual capabilities to decide applicability. Omit login design when login
is outside scope and billing design when the scope excludes paid services.
Record unknown scope, missing material, and unavailable environments as gaps.

### 2. Check structure and references

Check required fields, placeholders, duplicate IDs, broken references, source
identity, object versions, and missing requirements and platform combinations.
Record actual structural execution. For an entry such as “show an error,”
verify the concrete message and retained state at L2.

### 3. Walk through the complete user task

Follow entry, user action, system feedback, state and data changes, outcome,
or exit on the normal path and applicable branches. Compare feature cards,
pages, rules, and acceptance. Trace new features, permissions, costs, and
outbound data back to their sources.

Use another reviewer or review round where appropriate and retain disagreements
and locators. Resolve them with originals, applicable facts, actual observation,
or user decisions. Attribute repeated review by the same model accurately.

### 4. Route unknowns

| Unknown | Example | Next action | Closure evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Verifiable fact | Platform interaction constraint, service support, or error meaning. | Read a primary source and record version and applicability. | Locatable support for the fact and an explanation of any conflict. |
| User tradeoff | Undoable deletion, overwrite or save as, transfer, or cost. | Present options, consequences, and a recommendation where relevant. Ask the highest-impact individual question using the current clarification policy. | Effective decision for the current version. |
| User problem or outcome hypothesis | Task difficulty, comprehension, or time saved. | Design an applicable interview, observation, or prototype task. Perform only authorized research. | Actual record, preset criterion, sample, and limits. |
| Technical feasibility | A target platform can meet a promise or restore data. | Link the technical issue and verification card. | Required technical fact or actual trial. |
| Environment, permission, or budget blocker | No target environment, permission, or usable quota. | Retain the blocker and continue independent work. | Valid prerequisites and completion of the original required checks. |

Use web references for methods and scoped facts. Establish product tradeoffs
and claims about this project's users from their own evidence. Keep failures
and counterexamples from research and trials.

### 5. Prepare an evidence-based revision

Record original wording, finding, proposal, source or observation, applicability,
cost, and affected objects. Retain user requirements, suggestions, and unknowns
separately. Keep formal wording until an applicable decision changes it.

A research or prototype card includes question or hypothesis ID, related
decision, method and sample or recruitment scope, actual environment and
version, authorization and data handling, prerequisites and task, predefined
observations and judgment method, original record, result, limitations, and
next action. Report participants, interviews, satisfaction, timing, and
success rates only when supported by actual evidence. Use a walkthrough for
low-risk copy questions where suitable. Hold dependent work when a required
behavior hypothesis lacks its necessary evidence.

### 6. Recheck impact and obtain applicable confirmation

Preserve old and new versions and trace affected requirements, journeys, pages,
features, data, acceptance, technical interfaces, and tasks. Reassess on rule
or scope changes, counterexamples, and stale sources. Show material changes to
requirements, design, or permissions and obtain applicable confirmation.
Follow the saved workflow and current user decisions for confirmation.

Check copy changes for meaning. Reuse unrelated research or trials with a
documented impact assessment. Keep original evidence bound to its version
and environment. Mark affected objects pending recheck until checked.

### 7. Report and conclude the round

Report the version, scope, PC levels, evidence, issues, blockers, claim support,
actual research or trials, platform gaps, confirmation, budget, and next action.
Use scoring to identify weak areas and report blockers, value evidence, and
confirmation independently. State the actual scope of the outcome. A draft can
need work, design can be ready for handoff, affected tasks can be blocked, a
decision can be pending, or work can pause on environment or budget.

