### 4.1 Judgment boundaries

- Attribute a user's request to that user. Verify wider claims about similar users separately.
- PC01 and PC11 require an honest evidence level. Keep an unverified assumption unsatisfied or unverified if it is required for handoff.
- PC12 covers acceptance design. Record actual test results separately.
- PC15 covers the issue log. An accurate record can satisfy that condition while a required issue remains open and blocking.
- Use non-applicability only with a scope basis. Keep missing material, incomplete work, unavailable environments, and difficult checks in the applicable inventory.
- Attribute external practices and record the adoption reason and impact before treating them as project rules.

## 5. Issues, closure, reopening, and blockers

### 5.1 Issue card

Record issue ID, PC ID and level, object version, original wording and locator,
requirement, finding, evidence, affected requirements, pages, features,
acceptance, technical objects and tasks, severity rationale, blocked scope,
action type, owner, next action, closure condition, status, diff, recheck,
necessary confirmation, and history. Track pending, investigating, awaiting
decision, environment-blocked, revising, awaiting recheck, and closed. A
failure, evidence gap, or missing decision retains the applicable open state.

### 5.2 Closure by issue type

| Type | Closure requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Structural gap | Current required fields or references are correct and checked. |
| Unclear or conflicting product rule | Current design is clear and consistent, affected objects were reviewed, and material tradeoffs have applicable confirmation. |
| User or outcome hypothesis | Obtain the predefined evidence or an effective decision that changes goal or scope. Retain research limitations. |
| Technical feasibility conflict | Obtain required facts or actual trials on the related technical issue. Confirm changes to product, design, or permissions. |
| Permission, cost, or environment | Establish valid prerequisites and complete the originally required check. |

Keep the rationale and links for false positives, duplicates, and inapplicable
conditions. Preserve adverse findings. Reopen on a material counterexample and
retain the old closure history and new evidence.

### 5.3 Block by actual impact

| Severity | Examples and action |
| --- | --- |
| Critical | Goal or baseline conflicts, a broken core path, unauthorized transfer or cost, possible significant data loss, or infeasible core commitment. Block affected objects and dependencies. Obtain decisions on requirement, design, or permission changes. |
| Important | A concrete feature, state, rule, or acceptance gap prevents reliable implementation or judgment. Block dependent tasks and continue independent work. |
| General | Noncritical wording or maintenance issue with behavior and basis established. Record revision and the reason for progression. A user-defined required condition remains binding. |

Determine severity by actual consequence. Analyze unknown impact before
reclassifying it. Follow real dependencies when propagating blockers.

## 6. Versions, confirmation, budget, and recovery

### 6.1 Stage checkpoints

Use current confirmation policy. The four stages describe content maturity.
Use scope and final document confirmation for standalone work under the
current default, follow explicit stage-by-stage requests, and follow the
saved workflow decision contract in an active run. The table describes the
historical stage-by-stage checkpoints when applicable.

| Stage | Objects and review focus | Confirmation boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Initialization | Users, problems, goals, scope, platforms, and constraints. Focus on PC01–PC04. | Confirm the current initialization version and retain unknowns. |
| Requirements discovery | Scenarios, tasks, candidate requirements, sources, assumptions, and issues. | Confirm the current discovery result. Keep candidate requirements identifiable. |
| Requirements modeling | Journeys, feature cards, rules, states, acceptance, and traceability. Focus on PC05–PC13. | Confirm the current model and retain blockers for key unresolved rules. |
| Planning | Quality, product and technical handoff, tasks, and verification design. Focus on PC12–PC15. | Confirm the current plan and verify execution prerequisites separately. |

Use genuine user decisions for applicable approvals and follow required stage
progression independently of the document score.

### 6.2 Version impact

For changed requirements, scope, or rules, recheck journeys, features, pages,
acceptance, technical objects, and tasks. Check whether copy changes affect
meaning and comprehension evidence. Reassess interaction, data, cost, and
environment on platform, external-service, or permission changes. Reopen on
new user counterexamples or source conflicts.

Keep confirmations bound to the original version. Obtain applicable new
confirmation for affected changes. Reuse evidence after documented impact
assessment and retain the original execution or research version and limits.

### 6.3 Stop and resume

Conclude when applicable review and reporting finish. Pause affected actions
when a user decision is needed, the user pauses or cancels, a prerequisite
expires, an effective time, cost, request, output, or resource limit is reached,
or the next action may exceed the remaining budget. When repetition produces
no new evidence, follow the effective stop rule and preserve results.

Use the actual user authority and budget for research, trials, or contact
with others. Count failed calls, retries, and resources under the effective
rules. Keep unknown charges or limits visible. On resume, check baseline
changes, decisions, permission, environment, and remaining budget. Recheck
invalidated objects and continue unresolved issues. The historical design
round records no paid calls or real research.

## 7. Reporting and scoring boundaries

Use the [PM scoring rubric](product-scoring-rubric.md) only for an explicitly
requested score. For each PC check record rule, object inventory, applicability,
required and actual levels, status, evidence locator, issue, blocked scope,
and next action. Count the fixed objects and sub-items for coverage.

Report document design score, user and value evidence, blockers, confirmation,
and actual passes independently. Give each level its own evidence. Retain
required missing and unverified objects in the denominator. Keep critical
blockers visible at any score. Apply the historical 95-point proposal only
where the project adopts it. Report actual scores and product claims only
from corresponding evidence.

## 9. Methods and sources

The IDs, statuses, blocker and handling rules are local design. External
sources support methods. Obtain authority, project thresholds, and stage
decisions from the current project.

### Local basis

- The historical SOP checklist maps Q07 to user, problem, and scope, Q08 to feature behavior, Q09 to assessable quality, Q13 to consistency, Q16 to acceptance design, and Q21 to complete user tasks. Use Q05/Q06/Q14/Q17/Q18/Q22/Q23 as applicable.
- The Product SOP template supplies feature cards, traceability, acceptance cards, complete user tasks, and version and stage records in the selected document locale.
- Technical self-check design 0.2 supplies level, issue lifecycle, version, blocker, and stop methods. Review product and technical objects separately and link handoff. The frozen provenance preserves local source identities.

### External method mapping

The historical study recorded online reads of PS01–PS06 on 2026-09-30. Use
the source index in the frozen provenance for recorded titles, URLs, and
adoption boundaries. Attribute a fresh read only after actual access.

| Source ID | Primary source and method adopted | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| PS01 | [Atlassian Product Requirements template](https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence/templates/product-requirements) covers goals and success metrics, assumptions, user stories, priorities, design links, open items, and exclusions. It supports organization for PC01/PC02/PC03/PC04/PC06. | Use project evidence for goals and metrics. |
| PS02 | [Basecamp Write the Pitch](https://basecamp.com/shapeup/1.5-chapter-06) covers problems, appetite, solution, risks, and exclusions for PC01/PC03/PC09. | Use the current project's schedule and budget. |
| PS03 | [NASA guidance on writing a good requirement](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/appendix-c-how-to-write-a-good-requirement/) covers clarity, verifiability, source, and assumptions for PC04/PC07/PC08/PC12/PC13. | Feature fields and check IDs are local design. |
